1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,155
"A laptop lost power during a commit, and
Git now says fatal: index file corrupt.

2
00:00:09,195 --> 00:00:12,923
What did we lose: the history, the
work in progress, or nothing?"

3
00:00:13,263 --> 00:00:20,461
"The release script stamps builds
by reading .git/refs/heads/main.

4
00:00:20,501 --> 00:00:28,795
On the new build image that file does
not exist. Is the repository damaged?"

5
00:00:29,135 --> 00:00:31,819
"git fsck reports a dangling
commit on the build server.

6
00:00:31,859 --> 00:00:34,153
Is data at risk? Should
somebody run a cleanup?"

7
00:00:34,493 --> 00:00:43,465
"We keep four hundred versions of a 16
MB evaluation set in one repository.

8
00:00:43,505 --> 00:00:45,988
Are we storing 6 GB?"

9
00:00:46,328 --> 00:00:50,026
None of these can be answered with the
commands of the earlier parts alone.

10
00:00:50,066 --> 00:00:52,369
They are questions about
how Git stores things.

11
00:00:52,709 --> 00:00:56,948
The short answers, which this part
of the course will earn one by one.

12
00:00:56,988 --> 00:01:01,900
The index is rebuilt with one command, and
only content that was staged and never

13
00:01:01,940 --> 00:01:06,913
committed needs a second look. The ref is
in packed-refs or in a reftable, and the

14
00:01:06,953 --> 00:01:11,926
script should have asked Git. "Dangling"
describes where git fsck started looking,

15
00:01:11,966 --> 00:01:15,594
not what will be deleted, and
the cleanup is the risky part.

16
00:01:15,634 --> 00:01:20,730
And probably not: a pack stores versions
that differ in a few lines as deltas, while

17
00:01:20,770 --> 00:01:22,564
every snapshot stays complete.

18
00:01:24,404 --> 00:01:29,756
Welcome back to Git and GitHub Deep Mastery.
Pull up a chair: this video opens Part

19
00:01:29,796 --> 00:01:34,564
4, Git internals. Since the first part
of the course you have had a model.

20
00:01:34,604 --> 00:01:39,567
Four object types: blobs for file content,
trees for directories, commits for

21
00:01:39,607 --> 00:01:43,401
snapshots, and tags. Names that
point at them, called refs.

22
00:01:43,441 --> 00:01:47,429
And a staging area, the index.
In this part you open each box.

23
00:01:47,769 --> 00:01:52,703
I'll say this once for the part: the replays
use the fixed lab clock, so the commit

24
00:01:52,743 --> 00:01:57,497
IDs on screen equal the IDs in the book
and the ones you'll get when you replay.

25
00:01:57,837 --> 00:02:03,082
Today, two things. The .git directory, file
by file: what git init creates, what the

26
00:02:03,122 --> 00:02:08,051
first commit adds, and which of those
files Git can rebuild and which it can't.

27
00:02:08,091 --> 00:02:12,455
And the smallest unit of storage:
one loose object, down to its bytes.

28
00:02:12,495 --> 00:02:16,921
You'll recompute an object ID with a
tool that knows nothing about Git.

29
00:02:16,961 --> 00:02:18,431
Hold on to that promise.

30
00:02:18,771 --> 00:02:22,196
Two habits come out of this
chapter, and both begin today.

31
00:02:22,236 --> 00:02:27,156
Read repository state through plumbing
commands, the low-level commands with stable

32
00:02:27,196 --> 00:02:31,159
output, because the files underneath
exist in more than one format.

33
00:02:31,199 --> 00:02:36,118
And before you delete or repair anything
under .git, decide whether it's primary or

34
00:02:36,158 --> 00:02:36,895
derived data.

35
00:02:38,735 --> 00:02:43,213
name every entry of a fresh .git directory
and say what reads or writes it;

36
00:02:43,553 --> 00:02:47,628
say which files of .git can be deleted
without breaking the repository;

37
00:02:47,968 --> 00:02:51,546
describe the bytes of a loose object:
header, content, compression;

38
00:02:51,886 --> 00:02:56,770
compute a blob's object ID by hand and
explain why the compression level does not

39
00:02:56,810 --> 00:02:57,195
matter;

40
00:02:57,535 --> 00:02:59,925
find the file that holds a given object.

41
00:03:01,765 --> 00:03:06,993
The .git directory. In one sentence: the
.git directory is the repository: an object

42
00:03:07,033 --> 00:03:12,137
database, names for objects, a log of how
those names moved, one staging file, and

43
00:03:12,177 --> 00:03:17,092
settings. The files next to it are a
working tree that Git can rebuild from it.

44
00:03:17,432 --> 00:03:22,632
Now precisely. A repository is either a .git
directory at the top of a working tree,

45
00:03:22,672 --> 00:03:27,873
or a bare directory without one. A .git
that's a plain file containing gitdir: and a

46
00:03:27,913 --> 00:03:32,802
path points at a repository stored
elsewhere, which is how linked worktrees and

47
00:03:32,842 --> 00:03:33,800
submodules work.

48
00:03:34,140 --> 00:03:39,424
You saw both in the last part. The official
data model names four kinds of data

49
00:03:39,464 --> 00:03:42,861
inside: objects, references,
the index and reflogs.

50
00:03:42,901 --> 00:03:47,848
Everything else is configuration or
the state of an operation in progress.

51
00:03:47,888 --> 00:03:51,891
Ask Git where the directory is
with git rev-parse --git-dir.

52
00:03:51,931 --> 00:03:53,239
Never assume ./.git.

53
00:03:53,579 --> 00:03:59,243
Primary or derived. "Primary" means that
Git can't recreate the file from anything

54
00:03:59,283 --> 00:04:05,087
else. Objects, loose or packed, are primary.
Refs, HEAD, config, hooks, info/exclude

55
00:04:05,127 --> 00:04:10,791
and reflogs are primary. The lookup tables
beside a pack, the .idx and .rev files,

56
00:04:10,831 --> 00:04:16,008
are derived from the pack. The index
is derived, except for what is staged.

57
00:04:16,048 --> 00:04:20,391
COMMIT_EDITMSG is scratch.
description is unused by Git itself.

58
00:04:20,731 --> 00:04:24,897
That column answers the objective
"which files can be deleted".

59
00:04:24,937 --> 00:04:29,438
Derived data is rebuilt. Primary
data is restored from another copy.

60
00:04:29,478 --> 00:04:35,046
And three kinds of primary data are never
cloned or pushed: hooks, info/exclude, and

61
00:04:35,086 --> 00:04:35,580
reflogs.

62
00:04:35,920 --> 00:04:40,632
The loose object. In one sentence: a
loose object is one compressed file whose

63
00:04:40,672 --> 00:04:45,689
content is a short header, a NUL byte and
the data, and whose file name is the hash

64
00:04:45,729 --> 00:04:50,624
of exactly those bytes. A NUL byte is a
byte whose value is zero, and a hash is a

65
00:04:50,664 --> 00:04:53,365
fixed-length fingerprint
computed from bytes.

66
00:04:53,705 --> 00:04:59,253
Now precisely, in the manual's three
sentences. The object is the prefix, type,

67
00:04:59,293 --> 00:05:05,123
space, size, NUL, followed by the data,
where the type is blob, tree, commit or tag

68
00:05:05,163 --> 00:05:10,710
and the size is the length of the data
in decimal. Prefix and data together are

69
00:05:10,750 --> 00:05:15,236
compressed with zlib, a standard
compression format, and stored.

70
00:05:15,276 --> 00:05:20,965
And "the object ID of the object is the
SHA-1 or SHA-256 (as appropriate) hash of

71
00:05:21,005 --> 00:05:22,592
the uncompressed data".

72
00:05:22,932 --> 00:05:27,584
The file sits under objects/, in a directory
named after the first two hexadecimal

73
00:05:27,624 --> 00:05:28,557
digits of the ID.

74
00:05:28,897 --> 00:05:33,797
🟢 SAFE: git hash-object <file> computes
the ID that the file's content has as a

75
00:05:33,837 --> 00:05:38,988
blob, and writes the object with -w. It
hashes what git add would store: if a clean

76
00:05:39,028 --> 00:05:43,740
filter or a line-ending conversion
applies to the path, that's the converted

77
00:05:43,780 --> 00:05:46,679
content. --no-filters
hashes the file as it is.

78
00:05:47,019 --> 00:05:53,296
Inside .git, git hash-object -w creates
one new file under objects/, read-only.

79
00:05:53,336 --> 00:05:57,534
Nothing else: no index entry,
no ref, no reflog line.

80
00:05:57,874 --> 00:06:02,237
Why does the compression level not
matter? Because the ID is the hash of the

81
00:06:02,277 --> 00:06:06,698
uncompressed bytes. The compression is
a storage detail and isn't part of the

82
00:06:06,738 --> 00:06:07,220
identity.

83
00:06:07,559 --> 00:06:12,684
When should you not work at this level? In
scripts that need a branch's value or a

84
00:06:12,724 --> 00:06:16,651
file's content. The loose form
is one of several storage forms.

85
00:06:16,691 --> 00:06:21,815
Use plumbing, git rev-parse, git for-each-ref,
git cat-file, git ls-files, and let

86
00:06:21,855 --> 00:06:23,012
Git find the bytes.

87
00:06:24,852 --> 00:06:27,574
The textbook has two
analogies, one for each half.

88
00:06:27,914 --> 00:06:32,898
For the directory: a records office. The
vault holds sealed documents filed under a

89
00:06:32,938 --> 00:06:37,740
number computed from their content: objects.
A card index says which document is

90
00:06:37,780 --> 00:06:42,583
current for each project: refs. A visitors'
book records every change to a card:

91
00:06:42,623 --> 00:06:47,667
reflogs. One tray on the clerk's desk holds
the draft of the next filing: the index.

92
00:06:47,707 --> 00:06:52,268
The analogy breaks in one place that
matters. A sealed document can never be

93
00:06:52,308 --> 00:06:57,110
amended, not even by the office. Every
"change" files a new document and moves a

94
00:06:57,150 --> 00:06:57,413
card.

95
00:06:57,753 --> 00:07:01,975
For the object: a sealed envelope filed
under a number that's computed from what is

96
00:07:02,015 --> 00:07:06,135
inside it. Two envelopes with the same
content get the same number, so the office

97
00:07:06,175 --> 00:07:06,649
keeps one.

98
00:07:06,989 --> 00:07:12,391
An envelope whose content differs by
one character gets an unrelated number.

99
00:07:12,431 --> 00:07:17,905
This analogy breaks at correction: a
real archive can fix a filing mistake by

100
00:07:17,945 --> 00:07:23,490
relabelling, and here the label can't be
changed without changing the content.

101
00:07:25,330 --> 00:07:30,234
Four numbered lines. Everything else in the
directory is settings or the state of an

102
00:07:30,274 --> 00:07:34,766
operation in progress. The rest of this
part takes these lines one at a time.

103
00:07:35,106 --> 00:07:40,426
Sixteen bytes of content. A header of seven
characters, "blob 16", and one NUL byte:

104
00:07:40,466 --> 00:07:45,019
twenty-four bytes. Two things are
computed from those twenty-four bytes,

105
00:07:45,059 --> 00:07:50,187
independently: the hash, which is the ID,
and the compressed stream, which is the

106
00:07:50,227 --> 00:07:54,271
file. The left arrow never looks
at the output of the right one.

107
00:07:58,611 --> 00:08:02,860
Predict: in a repository with
no commit, is there an index?

108
00:08:02,900 --> 00:08:06,132
A ref? An object? Say
it out loud. I'll wait.

109
00:08:10,972 --> 00:08:15,096
No object, no ref, no index. The listing
leaves out the fourteen sample hooks.

110
00:08:15,136 --> 00:08:19,260
refs/heads is an empty directory, and
yet the repository already has a current

111
00:08:19,300 --> 00:08:19,634
branch.

112
00:08:19,974 --> 00:08:24,336
HEAD names refs/heads/main, a ref that
doesn't exist yet: an "unborn" branch, which

113
00:08:24,376 --> 00:08:28,685
is legal. The first commit creates the ref.
In config, repositoryformatversion = 0

114
00:08:28,725 --> 00:08:33,088
says that no extension is in use. ignorecase
was probed from this Mac's filesystem,

115
00:08:33,128 --> 00:08:37,437
and precomposeunicode is set on macOS.
description is read by the gitweb interface

116
00:08:37,477 --> 00:08:40,195
and by one sample hook, and
no Git command needs it.

117
00:08:40,535 --> 00:08:41,698
Now the first commit.

118
00:08:42,038 --> 00:08:47,262
A quick quiz: two files in two directories.
How many objects does the first commit

119
00:08:47,302 --> 00:08:50,022
write: two, three or
five? Say it out loud.

120
00:08:54,862 --> 00:09:00,069
Five. Two files in the working tree became
five objects: two blobs, the tree for

121
00:09:00,109 --> 00:09:05,512
src, the top-level tree, and the commit.
Three things appeared besides the objects:

122
00:09:05,552 --> 00:09:09,251
the index, the ref refs/heads/main,
and two reflog files.

123
00:09:09,291 --> 00:09:10,497
And COMMIT_EDITMSG.

124
00:09:13,837 --> 00:09:18,288
If you said two or three, you counted the
files and forgot the directories and the

125
00:09:18,328 --> 00:09:20,041
commit, which is the usual slip.

126
00:09:20,381 --> 00:09:26,044
The whole branch is one line of 41 bytes:
forty hexadecimal digits and a newline.

127
00:09:26,084 --> 00:09:29,636
It holds 08ffd04, the commit
you made a moment ago.

128
00:09:29,676 --> 00:09:35,410
The reflog line reads: old value, all zeros
because the ref didn't exist, then new

129
00:09:35,450 --> 00:09:39,565
value, who, when, and why. git
count-objects confirms five.

130
00:09:51,905 --> 00:09:57,299
Without -w, an ID is printed and nothing
is stored: the first find prints nothing.

131
00:09:57,339 --> 00:10:02,800
With -w, one file appears. Look at its path:
the directory f7, the first two digits

132
00:10:02,840 --> 00:10:06,909
of the ID, and a file name made
of the remaining thirty-eight.

133
00:10:07,249 --> 00:10:10,993
The object file isn't the working
tree file. Decompress it.

134
00:10:11,333 --> 00:10:15,517
The content is sixteen bytes. Predict
the bytes that come before it.

135
00:10:15,557 --> 00:10:16,138
I'll wait.

136
00:10:20,978 --> 00:10:26,349
Read the header bytes aloud:
b, l, o, b. A space. One, six.

137
00:10:26,389 --> 00:10:32,128
Then \x00, the NUL byte. Then the
content, ending in a newline.

138
00:10:32,168 --> 00:10:34,237
wc -c confirms sixteen.

139
00:10:34,577 --> 00:10:37,952
And the last command. shasum
knows nothing about Git.

140
00:10:37,992 --> 00:10:43,106
You hand it the same twenty-four bytes
with printf, and it prints the same forty

141
00:10:43,146 --> 00:10:46,843
digits: f784b584 and the rest.
That's all an object ID is.

142
00:10:46,883 --> 00:10:47,681
Promise kept.

143
00:10:48,021 --> 00:10:51,387
Notice also what wasn't involved
in that computation: zlib.

144
00:10:51,427 --> 00:10:54,735
The ID was computed from bytes
that were never compressed.

145
00:10:55,075 --> 00:11:00,144
Try it now, thirty seconds, in any terminal.
Type that printf line, pipe it into

146
00:11:00,184 --> 00:11:04,358
shasum, and predict the first four
digits before you press return.

147
00:11:04,398 --> 00:11:04,997
I'll wait.

148
00:11:09,837 --> 00:11:15,780
You should see f784, on your machine as
on mine, because the ID depends only on

149
00:11:15,820 --> 00:11:16,688
those bytes.

150
00:11:17,028 --> 00:11:22,239
-t prints the type, -s the size of the
data without the header, -p the content.

151
00:11:22,279 --> 00:11:26,958
Then three experiments. Another name
in another directory: the same ID.

152
00:11:26,998 --> 00:11:31,544
The same bytes from standard input,
with no file at all: the same ID.

153
00:11:31,584 --> 00:11:36,928
One changed byte, a 4 for a 3: an ID that
has nothing in common with the old one.

154
00:11:36,968 --> 00:11:42,444
The ID depends on nothing except the content.
Not the file name, not the directory,

155
00:11:42,484 --> 00:11:45,701
not the time, not the
author, not the repository.

156
00:11:46,041 --> 00:11:50,340
One more distinction. An object in
the database isn't a tracked file.

157
00:11:50,380 --> 00:11:55,559
Predict what git status says about config.toml,
whose blob you stored a moment ago.

158
00:11:55,899 --> 00:12:00,220
Untracked. Tracking is a matter of the
index, and the index is still empty.

159
00:12:00,260 --> 00:12:05,045
git add then created the index entry and
wrote no second object: the blob it needed

160
00:12:05,085 --> 00:12:08,359
was already there, under the
name it would have computed.

161
00:12:10,199 --> 00:12:11,847
Five mistakes to watch for.

162
00:12:12,187 --> 00:12:17,367
A script that reads .git/refs/heads/main.
Root cause: a ref can be stored as a loose

163
00:12:17,407 --> 00:12:22,402
file, in packed-refs, or in a reftable;
only plumbing such as git rev-parse reads

164
00:12:22,442 --> 00:12:23,023
all forms.

165
00:12:23,363 --> 00:12:29,365
Assuming the repository is at ./.git.
Root cause: in a linked worktree or a

166
00:12:29,405 --> 00:12:35,971
submodule, .git is a file that points
elsewhere; git rev-parse --git-dir gives the

167
00:12:36,011 --> 00:12:36,697
location.

168
00:12:37,037 --> 00:12:44,417
Deleting something under .git without
asking "primary or derived?".

169
00:12:44,457 --> 00:12:53,499
Root cause: an index or a pack index can
be rebuilt; an object or a reflog cannot.

170
00:12:53,839 --> 00:12:57,291
Hashing the file's bytes alone
and getting a different ID.

171
00:12:57,331 --> 00:13:02,108
Root cause: the ID is the hash of header,
NUL and content, not of the content by

172
00:13:02,148 --> 00:13:02,529
itself.

173
00:13:02,869 --> 00:13:06,352
Believing that storing an
object makes a file tracked.

174
00:13:06,392 --> 00:13:11,636
Root cause: tracking is an index entry;
git hash-object -w writes one object file

175
00:13:11,676 --> 00:13:12,745
and nothing else.

176
00:13:14,585 --> 00:13:20,204
Now, out of the lab. A release pipeline
for an inference service stamps each build

177
00:13:20,244 --> 00:13:24,689
with the commit of main by reading
the file .git/refs/heads/main.

178
00:13:24,729 --> 00:13:29,451
After the build image is updated,
the stamp step fails: no such file.

179
00:13:29,491 --> 00:13:32,970
The on-call engineer's first
thought is corruption.

180
00:13:33,310 --> 00:13:38,220
A colleague who knows the layout runs one
plumbing command, git rev-parse on the

181
00:13:38,260 --> 00:13:42,613
branch name, and gets the commit ID
at once. The repository is healthy.

182
00:13:42,653 --> 00:13:47,500
The ref is stored in another form on that
image, and the loose file was never a

183
00:13:47,540 --> 00:13:52,635
contract. The script is changed to ask Git,
and the review comment quotes the first

184
00:13:52,675 --> 00:13:53,935
habit of the chapter.

185
00:13:54,275 --> 00:13:59,427
The same week a container image for a
different service is found to include .git.

186
00:13:59,467 --> 00:14:04,620
The team's security note uses the other
lesson from section 3.2: the directory is

187
00:14:04,660 --> 00:14:09,684
the whole history. An image, a CI cache
or a backup that includes .git includes

188
00:14:09,724 --> 00:14:15,005
every version of every file, also the
credentials file that was "deleted" two years

189
00:14:15,045 --> 00:14:15,261
ago.

190
00:14:15,601 --> 00:14:20,480
Content addressing pays the team back in
three ways, as the textbook lists them.

191
00:14:20,520 --> 00:14:24,537
Deduplication: the same lock file
on forty branches is one object.

192
00:14:24,577 --> 00:14:29,579
Integrity: an ID is a checksum, so a copy
of an object from any clone or backup is

193
00:14:29,619 --> 00:14:34,374
known to be the right bytes if its ID
matches. Idempotence: recording the same

194
00:14:34,414 --> 00:14:39,416
content twice changes nothing. You relied
on the second property in every recovery

195
00:14:39,456 --> 00:14:40,461
of the last part.

196
00:14:42,301 --> 00:14:49,100
Your turn. Do Exercise 16.1, Level
1, "An object ID by hand", in

197
00:14:49,140 --> 00:14:52,413
exercises/m16-m18-internals.md.

198
00:14:52,753 --> 00:14:56,895
Before you run git hash-object, write down
the exact bytes you'll hash: the type

199
00:14:56,935 --> 00:15:01,130
word, the size in decimal, where the NUL
goes, and whether your content ends in a

200
00:15:01,170 --> 00:15:04,111
newline. Predict the path of
the object file from the ID.

201
00:15:04,151 --> 00:15:08,398
Work in labs/shell. The object ID of a
given content is the same on every machine,

202
00:15:08,438 --> 00:15:10,855
although commit IDs there
differ from the book.

203
00:15:11,195 --> 00:15:17,032
The challenge is Exercise 17.9, Level 4,
"Not a git repository", in the same file.

204
00:15:20,872 --> 00:15:23,413
Answer out loud, with
a concrete small file.

205
00:15:23,753 --> 00:15:28,601
A strong answer writes out the byte sequence
for a specific content, field by field,

206
00:15:28,641 --> 00:15:33,489
including the two bytes people forget: the
separator after the size and the trailing

207
00:15:33,529 --> 00:15:38,318
newline of the content if there is one. It
names a tool outside Git that reproduces

208
00:15:38,358 --> 00:15:38,725
the ID.

209
00:15:39,065 --> 00:15:41,804
It explains the order of operations in
storage, hashing and compression as two

210
00:15:41,844 --> 00:15:44,618
independent computations on the same
input, and concludes about the compression

211
00:15:44,658 --> 00:15:47,575
level from that. It may add what else the
ID doesn't depend on, and one consequence

212
00:15:47,615 --> 00:15:48,537
that matters in production.

213
00:15:50,377 --> 00:16:00,615
Let's land this. Bytes in, ID out, and
nothing else counts: not the file name, not

214
00:16:00,655 --> 00:16:04,501
the place, not the compression.

215
00:16:04,841 --> 00:16:23,557
You should now be able to say:

216
00:16:23,897 --> 00:16:29,352
.git holds four kinds of data, objects,
refs, reflogs and the index, plus settings

217
00:16:29,392 --> 00:16:32,032
and the state of operations in progress.

218
00:16:32,372 --> 00:16:35,950
A fresh repository has HEAD naming an unborn
branch and no object, ref or index; the

219
00:16:35,990 --> 00:16:38,534
first commit adds objects, the
index, a ref and two reflogs.

220
00:16:38,874 --> 00:16:42,617
Before I delete or repair anything under
.git I ask whether it is primary or

221
00:16:42,657 --> 00:16:44,757
derived, and I read
state through plumbing.

222
00:16:45,097 --> 00:16:49,580
A loose object is zlib-compressed "type,
space, size, NUL, content", stored under

223
00:16:49,620 --> 00:16:53,154
objects/ in a directory named by
the first two digits of its ID.

224
00:16:53,494 --> 00:16:58,231
The object ID is the hash of the uncompressed
bytes, so it depends only on type,

225
00:16:58,271 --> 00:17:01,814
size and content, and not on
compression, file name or time.

226
00:17:03,654 --> 00:17:11,204
Read sections 3.1 to 3.3 of Chapter 3.
Repeat Lab 0.2, "Read every file in an empty

227
00:17:11,244 --> 00:17:17,697
.git", in lab-manual/m00-lab-setup.md,
and name every file from memory.

228
00:17:18,037 --> 00:17:22,959
Today you opened the dot git folder and
computed an object ID with a tool that knows

229
00:17:22,999 --> 00:17:26,799
nothing about Git. That's the
foundation for the whole of Part 4.

230
00:17:26,839 --> 00:17:31,643
Practise with the exercise before you move
on. Next time: the four object types in

231
00:17:31,683 --> 00:17:36,605
full, and reading objects with git cat-file.
Until then, look at the state first and

232
00:17:36,645 --> 00:17:38,790
type second. See you in the next one.
